A Jet test cannot compare a Jet Int result with an inline C Int result on the freshly built compiler. The test stops before execution. Found while answering whether examples can carry foreign-language reference comparisons. No compiler source was changed.

Reproduction package.jet:
```jet
name: "inline_ffi_compare"
version: "0.1.0"
authority: { holds: { allow: [FFI] } }
```

compare.jet:
```jet
fn double(n: Int) Int -> (n * 2)

#[Unsafe("scalar int64_t ABI; inputs restricted to -100 through 100"), FFI(c)]
fn c_double(n: Int) Int -> {
    """int64_t c_double(int64_t n) { return n * 2; }"""
}

#Test("Jet matches inline C") {
    loop n in -100..100 {
        #Unsafe("audited scalar call; bounded inputs cannot overflow int64_t") {
            assert(double(n) == c_double(n))
        }
    }
}

```

Run scripts/agent/jet-env cargo build -p jet --bin jet with CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0, then scripts/agent/jet-env full jet test --show-default --serial <scratch>/compare.jet. Keep scratch under ~/.cache/jet-test-scratch.

Observed 2026-09-11: ordinary assert equality exits 101 with `canonical MIR optimization failed: MIR pass legality-verification rejected the program: invalid MIR: MIR foreign call references missing ID 15921114138792170145`. Replacing that assertion with `assert_eq(double(n), c_double(n))` exits 101 earlier with `require_eq operands have different checked types`. Both report Source location crates/jet-driver/src/Driver/mod.rs:6525:33. Root causes are not yet established; these are two failures of the same comparison workflow.

Scope is making this existing FFI/test composition work, not adding a new comparison syntax or a benchmark feature.

Found by the Jetpack port (#3590, PkgModelFinish, Opus 5.5); reproduced by the lead with target/debug/jet built 2026-09-26 03:05. Command: systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --expand-environment=no -p MemoryMax=4G -p MemorySwapMax=0 timeout 300 target/debug/jet run src/<file>.jet (from the repro directory; package.jet beside src/). Repro ~/.cache/jet-luna/PkgModelFinish/defects/A-missing-target/src/missing_target.jet (9 lines):

struct Spec {
    fn measure(input: String) -> Int {
        input.trim().len()
    }
}
fn run() { print(Spec.measure(" x ")) }

jet check passes; jet run: "internal compiler error: checked TIR cannot lower to MIR at 0..87: missing checked function target src::src/missing_target.jet::Spec::measure", exit 101 (I2: a checked program must not ICE). Same ICE on AOT (jet test). Blocks every Jetpack type-body static method (RefSpec.classify_in, BuildEffect.parse, ProviderKind.parse, WorkspaceIndex.resolve, ...).

Found by the Jetpack port (#3590, PkgModelFinish, Opus 5.5); reproduced by the lead with target/debug/jet built 2026-09-26 03:05. Command: systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --expand-environment=no -p MemoryMax=4G -p MemorySwapMax=0 timeout 300 target/debug/jet run src/<file>.jet (from the repro directory; package.jet beside src/). Repro ~/.cache/jet-luna/PkgModelFinish/defects/G-assert-optional/src/assert_optional.jet:

fn code() -> ?String { Val("E1231") }
fn run() {
    assert_eq(code(), Val("E1231"))
    print("ok")
}

jet run: "internal compiler error: Cranelift cannot execute MIR function src::src/assert_optional.jet::run: MIR debug type ?String has no checked debug carrier", exit 101. Blocks ported tests that compare optional results.

Found by the Jetpack port (#3590, PkgModelFinish, Opus 5.5); reproduced by the lead with target/debug/jet built 2026-09-26 03:05. Command: systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --expand-environment=no -p MemoryMax=4G -p MemorySwapMax=0 timeout 300 target/debug/jet run src/<file>.jet (from the repro directory; package.jet beside src/). Repro ~/.cache/jet-luna/PkgModelFinish/defects/F-enum-constant/src/comptime_const.jet:

@COLORS :: [Color]{Color.Red}
enum Color { Red }
struct Holder { fn new() -> Int { 1 } }
fn run() { print(@COLORS.len()) }

jet run: E0956 "missing checked function target __comptime::Holder::new isn't supported by the current evaluator yet" and E0107 "Nothing named @COLORS exists here". Unrelated Holder.new breaks the comptime evaluation of an enum-list constant.

D-DOTCTOR3=A makes the `Type{ body }` head universal for every type, with the body elaborated like an expected-type position. The optional head is the way to give a binding an optional type, since Jet bindings carry no type annotation (owner, 2026-09-28: `limit := ?Int{Val(10)}`).

Observed with target/release/jet on 2026-09-28 (6fc6f3862 plus working tree):

```jet
fn run() {
    b :: ?Int{Val(4)}
    print(b ?? 0)
}
```

fails with E0003 "Expected a value, found `?`" at the `?`. Expected: the head parses and `b` is a present `?Int`; `?Int{None}` is the typed absent value.

This is a defect against a ratified rule, not a new surface. Per the owner's Rust-archive direction, fix it in the Jet compiler (Compiler/JetParser value grammar, then sema elaboration). Interacts with D-OPT-LIFT1: under its option A the body lifts, so `?Int{10}` is also present.

Observed 2026-09-28 with target/release/jet built from 6fc6f3862 plus the #3663 working-tree repairs.

```jet
#Error
enum DBError {
    Down
}

#Error
enum TimeoutError {
    Late
}

struct Entry {
    id: Int
}

fn fetch(id: Int) -> Entry !(DBError | TimeoutError) {
    if id < 0 -> return Err(DBError.Down)
    return Entry{id: id}
}

fn run() {
    entry :: fetch(3) ?? return
    print("got {entry.id}")
}
```

`jet run` fails with exit 101: `checked TIR cannot lower to MIR at 175..181: fetch: missing MIR owner MirTypeId(4703923276956177559)`. The same happens with `-> ?Entry !(DBError | TimeoutError)`. The single-error form `-> ?Entry !DBError` runs. Expected: prints `got 3`.

The whole-union contract is ratified surface (D-FAILURE-FOUNDATION1=A), so this is a defect. The Rust frontend is stage zero of the self-hosting bootstrap (#218), so it matters there too; the Jet compiler must not inherit it.

Observed 2026-09-28 with target/release/jet built from 6fc6f3862 plus the #3663 working-tree repairs.

```jet
#Error
enum StoreErr {
    Missing
}

impl StoreErr -> Err {
    return Err("store unavailable: missing user")
}

struct User {
    name: String
}

fn read_store() -> User !StoreErr {
    Err(StoreErr.Missing)
}

fn load_user() -> User {
    read_store()
}

fn run() {
    user :: load_user() ?? {
        print("load failed: {err.message}")
        return
    }
    print(user.name)
}
```

Prints `load failed: src/run.jet` instead of `load failed: store unavailable: missing user`. When unhandled, the same program's report correctly reads `Error: store unavailable: missing user (type: StoreErr)`, so the conversion runs but `err.message` in the fallback reads the wrong field. The run then hit the JIT drop ICE family fixed on #3663 (fallback cleanup frame).

D-PREPOST1 says #Pre/#Post conditions are pure. Observed 2026-09-28 (target/debug/jet 13:07, d4bb652df plus working tree):

```jet
fn noisy(n: Int) -> Bool {
    print("side effect")
    n > 0
}

#Pre(noisy(n), "n must be positive")
fn half(n: Int) -> Int {
    n * 2
}

fn run() {
    print(half(4))
}
```

`jet check` reports 0 problems; `jet run` prints `side effect` then `8`. Herb Sutter's C++26 contracts talk (CppCon 2025, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oitYvDe4nps, ~22:36) makes the same rule a compile-time error because a check may run zero or more times; Jet's proof-keyed erasure (a proven #Pre is removed) makes an effectful condition change program output depending on what the prover finds.

Found by the mine-for-jet playlist run (report mine-for-jet-2026-09-28).

What is broken: when a failure travels up through automatic propagation and
reaches the program edge, Jet prints only the message. Report finding F2,
probe p25:

    fn parse(raw: String) -> Int !Err {
        if raw == "" -> return Err("empty input")
        Ok(7)
    }
    fn double(raw: String) -> Int {
        n :: parse(raw)
        n * 2
    }
    fn total() -> Int {
        a :: double("x")
        b :: double("")
        a + b
    }
    fn run() {
        print("start")
        print(total())
        print("end")
    }

Output, with and without --verbose: `start` then `Error: empty input`, exit 1.
There is no file, line, or path. A learner cannot tell which call failed.

Why it matters: D-FAIL-MODEL1=A says every failure is one report with a code,
message, why, fix, and source location. D-ERRCTX1 says every fallible call
joins the failure journey automatically. Ko et al. (2004) call this an
information barrier: the learner has a hypothesis but no way to check it.

Owner instruction (chat, 2026-09-28): the owner reviewed the learnability report and directed that this fix be carded at P0. This card records that direction; it is not a ballot.

Probe sources live in the report's Appendix A. They ran from a directory with its own package.jet granting [IO, Mem.Alloc], on the working-tree debug binary of 2026-09-28 13:07 (HEAD 68e1f8fb plus another stream's uncommitted compiler edits). Re-run each probe on a fresh build before starting; a probe that no longer reproduces is recorded on the card, not silently dropped.

What breaks: inside `fn outer<P: Shape>(shape: P)`, calling `inner<P>(shape)` where `inner` is also `<P: Shape>` is rejected. The bound on the outer `P` is not visible when checking the inner call's bound.

Repro: Examples/bmp/scratch/repro/generic_bound_forward.jet

```jet
trait Shape { fn area(self) -> Int }
struct Square { side: Int  impl Shape { fn area(self) -> Int { self.side * self.side } } }
fn inner<P: Shape>(shape: P) -> Int { shape.area() }
fn outer<P: Shape>(shape: P) -> Int { inner<P>(shape) }
fn run() { print(outer(Square{side: 3})) }
```

Observed 2026-09-28: `Error [E0905]: P isn't Shape` on `jet build` and `jet run`. Writing `inner(shape)` without the explicit type argument instead reports `E0904: Can't figure out what P should be here` (seen in Examples/bmp/bmp_enhanced.jet while it used `write_bmp<P: Rows>`; that file now uses a trait object instead).

Expected: prints 9 on every tier.

Found while preparing examples for the owner's F1 discussion (2026-09-28). The owner is leaning toward required, formatter-inserted `~` markers, which depends on copy detection being exact. Today it is not. Probes ran on the working-tree debug binary of 2026-09-28 13:07.

Probe p31 (no policy):

    struct User {
        name: String
        tags: [String]
    }
    fn make(name: String) -> User {
        User{name: name, tags: [String]{}}
    }
    fn run() {
        first :: "Ada"
        names := [String]{}
        names.push(first)
        u :: make(first)
        label :: "  admin  ".trim()
        kept :: User{name: label, tags: names}
        print("{first} {names.len()} {u.name} {kept.name} {kept.tags.len()}")
    }

1. `jet audit copies p31_copies.jet` reports three implicit copies: 7:16 (`name` into the field, correct), 16:37 (`names` into `tags`, correct), and 1:8, which is the name `User` in `struct User {` and is not a copy site. Probe p33 shows the same bogus site at `struct Holder`.
2. `jet fmt --explicit-copies` uses those sites, inserts `~` before `User` in the struct declaration, and then refuses to write because the result does not parse. It reports E0003 errors with carets on the user's untouched source ("Expected a name after `struct`, found `~`"), which reads as if the user's code were broken.
3. Under `#Policy(copies: .Explicit)` (probes p32, p33), only the read-parameter-to-field copy (7:16, E0120) is reported. `kept :: User{name: label, tags: names}` and `b :: Holder{xs: a}` followed by `a.push(9)` copy silently (p33 prints `4 3`, so `b.xs` is an independent copy), even though `jet audit copies` itself classifies those sites as implicit copies. The policy's promise that copies are visible does not hold.
4. Minor: `jet audit p31_copies.jet` (without the `copies` word) ignores the file and fails looking for `run.jet`.

What is wrong: a function that omits its failure contract is implicitly fallible with the default Err (D-FAILURE-FOUNDATION1=A). Codegen treats that as 'always returns a Result', even when nothing inside the function can fail. Probe p34 (report Appendix, 2026-09-28, working-tree debug binary):

    fn build(title: String, rows: [String]) -> Report {
        Report{title: title, lines: rows}
    }

`jet emit --rust` lowers it to

    fn ...build(__jet_title: &String, __jet_rows: &Vec<String>) -> JetOutcome<...Report, JetErr>

and `load` and `run` likewise, although none of them contains a fallible operation. Every call then pays for wrapping, a branch, and a larger return value (Duffy's 'peanut butter' cost), and optimizers see more branches. AGENTS.md design priority 3: do not buy convenient syntax with hidden runtime overhead.

The owner's direction (chat, 2026-09-28): fix performance waste first, and infer wherever the meaning stays clear. Whether a function can fail is a fact the compiler already knows; the implicit beginner route must cost nothing when nothing can fail.

Minimal repro (jet-bootstrap-syntax-probe at target/debug, 2026-09-28):

fn b(socket: InheritedListener) -> Int {
    if socket == {
        .Unix{listener, ...} -> 1
        else -> 0
    }
}

Reports E0003 "Expected `->` after an `if` arm value or condition, found `{`" at the pattern, then knock-ons on `else` and `}`. D-CORE-NETINHERIT1 ratifies this spelling for matching InheritedListener. Jetpack keeps the canonical spelling (Jetpack/Store/Source/Broker/BrokerService.jet:93); no workaround. Fix: parse named-field variant patterns with an optional `...` rest in match arms, in the Jet parser (Compiler/JetParser) and the Rust bootstrap parser the probe uses.

Minimal repro (jet-bootstrap-syntax-probe, 2026-09-28; found by the Jetpack port):

struct Rec { version: String }
fn same(a: String?, b: Rec) -> Bool { a == Val(b.version) }

Reports E0003: the right operand of `==` is parsed as a pattern (field access `b.version` is not a pattern), although this is an ordinary optional equality in value position, not an `if x == {` match head. Comparing an optional against `Val(expr)` is the canonical spelling Jetpack uses. Fix in the parser: only a `==` followed by `{` starts a match; otherwise both operands are expressions. Jetpack keeps the canonical spelling; add affected lines to Jetpack/Bootstrap/known-syntax-defects.list with this card until fixed.
